  <!-- SIGNS YOU NEED A NEW CYLINDER -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Signs Your Liverpool Hot Water Cylinder Needs Replacing</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Most Liverpool homes with a system or regular boiler rely on a hot water cylinder. These are the signs that a swap is due.</p>

      <div class="pf-signs" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Running out of hot water often</strong><p>Does your home run out of hot water before everyone has showered? Then the tank is too small for your needs. A bigger tank or a swap to unvented gives more hot water at the same time.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Cylinder is leaking</strong><p>A leaking cylinder must be swapped. Cylinders cannot be fixed once the tank itself has gone. A small drip from a valve or fitting can sometimes be fixed without a swap. We find which it is on the visit.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Hot water takes a long time to reheat</strong><p>Does the tank take over 30 minutes to reheat? Often a failing immersion element. Or scale on the heat exchanger. Or a broken thermostat. We find the cause before we suggest a swap.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Cylinder is over 20 years old</strong><p>Most hot water cylinders last 15 to 25 years. An older one likely loses energy through the walls. Rust inside is more likely. And the risk of a sudden split rises. A planned swap avoids the water damage from a burst.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Poor hot water pressure across the home</strong><p>If hot water pressure is poor in your Liverpool home but cold pressure is fine, the cylinder type is the cause. A swap to unvented sorts this out. You get mains-pressure hot water at every tap.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pf-sign"><div><strong>Noisy cylinder or pipework</strong><p>Banging or kettling from the tank or pipes means scale on the immersion element. Or scale on the heat exchanger. Or air in the system. Often fixed with an element swap.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div style="background:#f97316;border-radius:10px;padding:1.5rem;text-align:center;margin-top:2rem;">
